(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"id":"c-g-01","n":901,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🔔","title":"Chest pain after a panic attack: examine the heart before you reassure","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 16:20, on call","where":"Female acute ward, standalone adult mental health unit","text":"The nurse bleeps you: a 61-year-old woman admitted a week ago with depression and panic disorder had a panic attack 20 minutes ago and still has central chest tightness. She smokes 20 a day and takes citalopram 20 mg and amlodipine 5 mg. She says, 'Nobody believes me about my heart.'"},"task":"Take a focused chest pain history, do a full cardiovascular examination, and decide between an emergency ambulance, same-day medical review or honest reassurance, with an ECG and troponin plan.","first60":["On the phone: 'Please do a full set of obs with NEWS2 and a 12-lead ECG now. I'm coming.'","Look before you touch: grey, sweaty, breathless or clutching her chest means call 999 (2222 on an acute hospital site) first.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, check name and date of birth, explain, get consent, offer a chaperone.","Sit her at 45 degrees; ask 'Any pain right now?' before you lay a hand on her.","Examine in order: hands, pulse, BP, face, neck, praecordium, lung bases, ankles; talk her through each step."],"ask":["'Can you point with one finger to where it hurts? Is it there now?'","'Did it start at rest or when you were moving about?'","'Does it spread to your arm, neck, jaw or back?'","'Is it tight or heavy, or sharp? Worse when you breathe in or when I press here?'","'Did you feel sick, sweaty or faint with it?'","'Any heart problems, diabetes, high cholesterol, or a clot in the leg or lung? Anyone in the family with a heart attack before 60?'","'Is this like your usual panic, or different?'","To the nurse: 'Any PRN today? Has she been mostly in bed this week?'"],"recognise":["Cardiac pattern: heavy or tight central pain, spreading to arm or jaw, with sweating, nausea or breathlessness, lasting more than 15 minutes or brought on by effort.","Panic can cause chest pain, but it is a diagnosis you reach after excluding the heart; a 61-year-old smoker can have both.","Hands: cool peripheries, capillary refill over 2 seconds, irregular pulse (AF), tar staining, tendon xanthomata.","Neck: JVP more than 3 cm above the sternal angle suggests right heart strain or failure; think pulmonary embolism.","Chest: displaced apex, new murmur, third heart sound, pericardial rub; fine basal crackles of pulmonary oedema.","Legs: pitting ankle oedema; one swollen, tender calf suggests DVT and possible PE.","Also consider PE (fast pulse, low sats, pleuritic pain), aortic dissection (tearing pain to the back, BP differs between arms), pneumothorax, reflux, chest wall pain."],"decide":[{"c":"ECG and obs now, full examination; if the ECG is abnormal, pain persists or obs are abnormal, call 999 and give aspirin 300 mg; if all normal and pain-free, send troponin and get same-day medical review before calling it panic.","r":"Safe: you exclude dangerous causes on evidence, so your reassurance is honest and she is more likely to accept it.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her it is anxiety, give PRN lorazepam and leave it for the day team.","r":"Unsafe: sedation masks symptoms, and a missed heart attack or PE on a mental health ward is a recurring serious incident.","best":false},{"c":"Send her to A&E in a taxi with a nurse without examining her.","r":"Wrong either way: suspected acute coronary syndrome needs an ambulance; if it is not that, a proper assessment may spare her a long wait in A&E.","best":false}],"law":["She is informal: she can agree to or refuse examination and transfer. If she refuses an ambulance, assess her capacity for that decision (MCA 2005) and record it.","If detained, the MHA 1983 doesn't cover treating a physical illness unrelated to her mental disorder; use her consent or the MCA.","A detained patient going to an acute hospital needs Section 17 leave and an escort plan; in an emergency follow the trust transfer policy and inform the responsible clinician straight away.","NICE CG95: suspected acute coronary syndrome with pain now, or pain in the last 12 hours plus an abnormal or unavailable ECG, needs emergency admission; a normal resting ECG does not exclude it."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm taking this seriously. I'm going to check your heart properly now: pulse, blood pressure, neck, chest and a heart tracing. Then I'll tell you exactly what I've found."},{"to":"Ambulance or medical registrar","s":"61-year-old woman, smoker, 40 minutes of central chest tightness after a panic attack. ECG shows new ST depression in V4 to V6, HR 104, BP 152/90, sats 97%. Aspirin 300 mg given. I'd like her transferred for suspected acute coronary syndrome."},{"to":"Patient (if everything is normal)","s":"Your heart tracing, pulse, blood pressure and heart sounds are normal. We'll still check a blood test for heart strain and a medical doctor will see you today. If it is clear, we'll look at the panic together."}],"write":"16:35 Called re chest pain. 61F, informal, depression and panic disorder.\nS: Central tightness 40 min after panic attack, no radiation, no sweating. Smoker 20/day, on amlodipine.\nO: HR 92 reg, BP 138/84 both arms, RR 18, sats 98% air, T 36.8, NEWS2 0. Warm peripheries, CRT <2 s, JVP not raised, apex undisplaced, HS I+II+0, chest clear, no oedema, calves soft.\nECG: sinus 90, no ST change, QTc 438 ms.\nA: Probable panic-related chest pain; ACS not yet excluded.\nP: hs-troponin 0 h and repeat per local pathway, FBC, U&E, lipids, glucose. Repeat ECG if pain recurs. Medical review today. Escalate via 999 if pain returns with ECG change or abnormal obs.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Hands: temperature, capillary refill (press 5 s, normal under 2 s), clubbing, tar staining; radial pulse rate and rhythm over 15 s; radio-radial delay.","Collapsing pulse: ask about shoulder pain first, then raise the arm while feeling the radial pulse with your palm.","BP in both arms; a difference over 20 mmHg raises the question of aortic dissection.","Face: conjunctival pallor, malar flush, mouth and teeth. Neck: JVP with head turned left at 45 degrees; carotids one side at a time.","Chest: inspect for scars; palpate apex (5th intercostal space, mid-clavicular line), heaves, thrills; listen in four areas with the diaphragm, then the bell at the apex with her rolled to the left; sit forward, listen at the left sternal edge in expiration.","Lung bases for crackles; press over the ankles and sacrum for oedema, watching her face; check calves.","Tests: 12-lead ECG (rhythm, ST change, new LBBB, QTc on citalopram), high-sensitivity troponin per local pathway, FBC, U&E, glucose, lipids."],"next":"Her ECG and examination are normal and the first troponin is in range. The medical registrar arranges the repeat troponin, which is also normal, and plans outpatient cardiology review. You explain this to her, then begin talking about how panic causes real chest sensations.","marks":["Got an ECG and NEWS2 started before arriving.","Took a focused pain history while examining.","Examined in a logical order with consent, chaperone offer and explanation at each step.","Checked BP in both arms and examined calves for DVT.","Did not label the pain as panic until cardiac causes were excluded.","Arranged troponin and medical review and documented a clear escalation trigger."],"pitfalls":["Giving PRN benzodiazepine as the first response to chest pain.","Trusting one normal ECG.","Examining in silence; anxious patients need running commentary.","Forgetting PE and dissection."],"refs":["1. NICE CG95 Recent-onset chest pain of suspected cardiac origin: assessment and diagnosis (2010, updated 2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Physicians. National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2 (2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG113 GAD and panic guideline (2011, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: citalopram and escitalopram, QT prolongation (2011; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: many UK mental health units are on separate sites with no on-site medical team or lab. Know whether your ward dials 999 or 2222, where the ECG machine and emergency bag are, and how bloods reach the lab out of hours.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Recorded a full set of obs with a NEWS2 score and a 12-lead ECG, with QTc noted","Completed a cardiovascular examination including BP in both arms, JVP, heart sounds, lung bases and calves","Stated whether acute coronary syndrome, PE or dissection is likely, with the findings that support it","Arranged troponin and same-day medical review, or an emergency ambulance with aspirin 300 mg given","Told her honestly what you found and what happens next"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I think I'm having a heart attack. Please don't just tell me it's nerves again.\"","r":"\"I won't. Chest pain needs checking properly, whatever else is going on. I'm going to examine you and do a heart tracing now, and I'll tell you what I find.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You're a psychiatrist. What do you know about hearts?\"","r":"\"Fair question. I'm a medical doctor first, and I'm trained to check for heart problems. If I find anything worrying, I'll get the medical team or an ambulance straight away.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't breathe, I can't breathe, it's happening again.\"","r":"\"I'm here with you. Your oxygen level is normal. Breathe out slowly with me, longer out than in. When it settles a little, I'll carry on checking your heart.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If the tracing's normal, does that mean my heart is fine?\"","r":"\"It's a good sign, but not the whole answer. A blood test called troponin shows heart strain, and sometimes we repeat it. When both are clear, we can be much more confident.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going in an ambulance. I'll lose my bed here.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to stay. Your tracing shows changes that need a hospital tonight. I'll ask the ward to keep your bed and phone the hospital ahead. Can I explain the risks of staying?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've had this pain walking up the stairs for a month. I didn't tell anyone.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's important. Pain on effort is a reason to see a heart specialist. I'll make sure the medical team knows today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["12-lead ECG now and with any new pain; high-sensitivity troponin at 0 h and repeat per local pathway; FBC, U&E, glucose, HbA1c, lipids","Suspected ACS: 999, aspirin 300 mg chewed (unless allergic), GTN 400 micrograms sublingual if systolic BP over 90; oxygen only if sats below 94%","Keep citalopram 20 mg if QTc under 450 ms; 40 mg is the maximum under 65","Smoking: nicotine patch 21 mg/24 h plus a short-acting product, offered today"],"psych":["Once the heart is cleared, explain the panic cycle: breathing, chest tightness, fear of a heart attack, more breathing","CBT for panic disorder (NICE CG113), weekly sessions with the ward or community psychology team"],"social":["With her consent, phone her daughter to explain the plan and the results","If transferred: nurse escort, copy of drug chart, ECG and notes summary go with her"],"safety":["Obs with NEWS2 4-hourly for 24 h; nurses to repeat ECG and call the doctor if pain returns","Escalation written in notes: new pain with ECG change or NEWS2 5 or more, call 999","Day team to review at handover and chase the troponin"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normal ECG does not exclude ACS","x":"A normal resting ECG lowers the odds but does not rule out acute coronary syndrome. Wait for the troponin and repeat the ECG with any further pain."},{"h":"Aspirin 300 mg, chewed","x":"For suspected acute coronary syndrome give a single 300 mg loading dose as soon as possible unless she is allergic; note the time on the chart and in the handover."},{"h":"Oxygen is not routine","x":"Give oxygen in suspected ACS only if sats are below 94% (88 to 92% target if she has COPD with CO2 retention)."},{"h":"Both arms, both calves","x":"A BP difference over 20 mmHg between arms suggests dissection; a swollen tender calf with tachycardia suggests PE. Thirty seconds each, and they change the plan."},{"h":"Citalopram and the QT","x":"Citalopram lengthens QTc with dose. Maximum 40 mg daily, 20 mg over 65. Check the QTc on every ECG you request."}]},
{"id":"c-g-02","n":902,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🛏","title":"Fever and green sputum three weeks into clozapine: examine the chest","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 18:10, on call","where":"Male acute ward, adult inpatient unit","text":"A 36-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia started clozapine three weeks ago and is now on 200 mg a day. Since this morning he has a cough with green sputum and feels 'rough'. Obs: T 38.3, HR 108, RR 22, sats 94% on air, BP 118/72, alert. NEWS2 is 5."},"task":"Do a full respiratory examination, grade severity, think about clozapine-specific dangers (neutropenia, myocarditis, toxicity) and decide on bloods, antibiotics, clozapine dosing and whether he needs the acute hospital.","first60":["NEWS2 of 5 needs an urgent doctor review: go now.","Ask the nurse for the clozapine chart and the last FBC date and result.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, consent, chaperone offer; sit him at 45 degrees with his chest exposed.","Examine: hands, pulse, RR, face, neck and trachea, chest front (expansion, percussion, auscultation), chest back, ankles.","Do a 12-lead ECG on the ward: myocarditis peaks in the first weeks of clozapine."],"ask":["'When did the cough start? What colour is the phlegm? Any blood?'","'Any pain when you breathe in? Where?'","'Any sore throat or mouth ulcers?'","'Any chest pain, racing heart or feeling short of breath lying flat?'","'Are you dribbling more at night? Have you choked on food or drink?'","'Are you still smoking? Any change since you came in?'","To the nurse: 'Any vomiting, drowsiness or fits? Is he constipated?'"],"recognise":["Consolidation: dull percussion, bronchial breathing, coarse crackles and increased vocal resonance over one area.","Severity: CRB-65 on the ward (confusion, RR 30 or more, systolic BP under 90 or diastolic 60 or less, age 65 or over); add urea for CURB-65 when bloods are back.","Neutropenia: sore throat, mouth ulcers or fever on clozapine means an urgent FBC; neutrophils below 1.5 is a red result.","Myocarditis: fever, tachycardia out of proportion, chest pain, breathlessness, raised troponin and CRP, usually in the first two months.","Aspiration: hypersalivation and sedation raise the risk; right lower lobe is the usual site.","Clozapine toxicity: infection and stopping smoking both raise levels; watch for drowsiness, seizures, hypotension, myoclonus.","Benign clozapine fever occurs in the first weeks but is only labelled once infection, neutropenia and myocarditis are excluded."],"decide":[{"c":"Examine, ECG, urgent FBC with neutrophils, CRP, U&E, troponin and clozapine level; hold tonight's clozapine until FBC and troponin are back; start amoxicillin if neutrophils are normal; transfer if NEWS2 rises to 7, sats fall below 92% or results are red.","r":"Correct: treats the pneumonia while excluding the three clozapine dangers, with clear transfer triggers.","best":true},{"c":"Start clarithromycin, continue clozapine and recheck in the morning.","r":"Risky: clarithromycin can raise clozapine levels, and you have not checked neutrophils or the heart.","best":false},{"c":"Stop clozapine permanently because he has a fever.","r":"Too hasty: fever alone is not a reason to stop for good. Act on the FBC and troponin; stopping without cause loses the only drug that works in resistant schizophrenia.","best":false}],"law":["Treatment of pneumonia is physical treatment: use his consent, or the MCA 2005 if he lacks capacity for that decision.","If detained and transferred to the acute hospital, he needs Section 17 leave and an escort plan agreed with the responsible clinician.","Clozapine must be dispensed with registration on the manufacturer's monitoring service; a red result means stop and do not restart without specialist advice."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've got a temperature and a chest infection is likely. I'm going to listen to your chest and check your heart tracing. Clozapine can lower your infection-fighting cells, so we need a blood test tonight."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Please hold tonight's clozapine until the FBC and troponin are back, recheck obs hourly, and call me if NEWS2 reaches 7 or his sats drop below 92%."},{"to":"Pharmacist (next morning)","s":"Clozapine week 3, 200 mg a day, pneumonia on amoxicillin. Dose held last night. Could you advise on restarting and whether to check a level?"}],"write":"18:30 Reviewed re fever. 36M, clozapine wk 3, 200 mg/day.\nS: Cough, green sputum since am, R-sided pleuritic pain. No sore throat. Hypersalivation at night.\nO: T 38.3, HR 108, RR 22, sats 94% RA, BP 118/72, alert. NEWS2 5. No clubbing. Trachea central. Reduced expansion R base, dull to percussion, coarse crackles and bronchial breathing R base. HS normal, no oedema.\nECG: sinus tachy 106, no ST change, QTc 432.\nA: R lower lobe pneumonia, CRB-65 0. Exclude neutropenia, myocarditis.\nP: FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, troponin, clozapine level. Clozapine held tonight. Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS once neutrophils known. Obs hourly. Transfer if NEWS2 7+, sats <92% or red FBC.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Hands: clubbing, tar staining, peripheral cyanosis, CO2 retention flap; pulse rate; count respirations for a full 30 s without telling him.","Face and neck: central cyanosis (tongue), Horner's, lymph nodes from behind, trachea position.","Chest front: scars, expansion with both thumbs, percussion side to side top to bottom, vocal resonance ('ninety-nine'), auscultation side to side including the axillae.","Chest back: ask him to sit forward and hug himself; repeat expansion, percussion, vocal resonance, auscultation.","Ankles for oedema; ECG; urgent FBC with neutrophils, CRP, U&E, LFT, troponin, clozapine level; sputum culture.","Chest X-ray via the acute hospital or next morning per local pathway."],"next":"FBC at 21:00: neutrophils 6.8, CRP 112, troponin normal. He starts amoxicillin. The next morning the pharmacist suggests restarting clozapine at a reduced dose and checking a level, as infection can push levels up.","marks":["Linked fever on clozapine to neutropenia, myocarditis and toxicity, not just pneumonia.","Systematic respiratory examination front and back.","Graded severity with NEWS2 and CRB-65.","Chose an antibiotic that does not interact with clozapine.","Agreed what to do with tonight's clozapine dose and set transfer triggers."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting the FBC: the first question with fever on clozapine.","Prescribing ciprofloxacin or erythromycin without checking interactions.","Missing more than 48 hours of clozapine without planning re-titration.","Examining only the front of the chest."],"refs":["1. NICE pneumonia guidance (check the current version) and your local antimicrobial policy; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Physicians. NEWS2 (2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and other antipsychotics, monitoring blood concentrations for toxicity (Aug 2020; checked Nov 2026)","4. Clozapine summary of product characteristics, haematological monitoring (electronic Medicines Compendium; checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, clozapine is supplied only while blood results are logged with the manufacturer's monitoring service (green, amber, red). Learn your trust's out-of-hours FBC route before you need it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Completed a respiratory examination front and back and named the affected lobe","Scored NEWS2 and CRB-65 and stated the transfer triggers in the notes","Sent FBC with neutrophils, CRP, troponin and a clozapine level, and done an ECG","Agreed tonight's clozapine dose with the nurse in charge and chosen a non-interacting antibiotic","Explained to the patient why the blood test matters tonight"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No more blood tests. I had one on Monday.\"","r":"\"I know, you've had lots. This one is different: with a fever on clozapine I need to check your infection-fighting cells tonight. It's one tube and it keeps you safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it the clozapine? Will you take me off it? It's the first thing that's worked.\"","r":"\"I hear how much it's helping. Most likely this is a chest infection. I may hold tonight's dose until the blood results are back, then we'll restart it carefully.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you need a heart tracing for a cough?\"","r":"\"Good question. Early on, clozapine can rarely inflame the heart, and it can look like flu. The tracing and a blood test let me rule that out.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me paracetamol and let me sleep it off.\"","r":"\"Paracetamol will help the fever, and you can have it. But your chest sounds infected, so you also need an antibiotic. Then sleep, and the nurses will check on you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped smoking when I came in. Haven't had one for a week.\"","r":"\"That's really useful to know, and well done. Stopping smoking raises clozapine levels, so I'll check your level and the pharmacist may adjust the dose.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Amoxicillin 500 mg three times a day for 5 days (low severity); penicillin allergy: doxycycline 200 mg on day 1 then 100 mg daily","Avoid ciprofloxacin and erythromycin or clarithromycin with clozapine unless the pharmacist agrees; they can raise levels","Hold clozapine pending FBC and troponin; if neutrophils below 1.5, stop and treat as possible neutropenic sepsis in the acute hospital","Clozapine level once settled; if more than 48 h of doses are missed, re-titrate from 12.5 mg","Paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day; hyoscine for hypersalivation only after review (adds constipation)"],"psych":["Reassure that clozapine is likely to continue; watch for relapse if doses are held","Nurses to record mental state each shift while doses are reduced or held"],"safety":["Obs hourly overnight; nurse to call if NEWS2 7 or more, sats below 92% or new confusion","Transfer to A&E with escort if any trigger is met","Ward pharmacist and day team to review clozapine at handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fever on clozapine: three questions","x":"Is he neutropenic? Is it myocarditis? Is the level rising? FBC, troponin with CRP and ECG, and a clozapine level answer them."},{"h":"Red means stop","x":"UK clozapine monitoring: neutrophils below 1.5 or white cells below 3.0 is red. Stop clozapine and seek specialist advice before any restart."},{"h":"Infection pushes levels up","x":"Inflammation and stopping smoking both slow clozapine breakdown. Levels can climb sharply in days; watch for sedation, hypotension and seizures."},{"h":"48 hours missed: start again","x":"If clozapine is missed for more than 48 hours, re-titrate from 12.5 mg once or twice daily to avoid hypotension and collapse."},{"h":"CRB-65 on the ward","x":"Confusion, RR 30 or more, low BP, age 65 or over: one point each. 0 is low risk; 2 or more means hospital assessment."}]},
{"id":"c-g-03","n":903,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🛏","title":"No bowel movement for 12 days on clozapine: examine the abdomen","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 11:30","where":"Rehabilitation ward, adult mental health unit","text":"A 47-year-old man on clozapine 450 mg a day for six years, plus hyoscine for drooling, tells the nurse he has not opened his bowels for 12 days. He vomited twice this morning and his tummy looks swollen. He also drinks about 6 cans of strong lager on leave. HR 104, BP 112/70, T 37.6."},"task":"Do a full abdominal examination, recognise obstruction or ileus and signs of liver disease, and decide on urgent surgical transfer and what to do with clozapine.","first60":["Treat this as a possible surgical emergency: clozapine gut slowdown can kill.","Ask the nurse: no laxatives, enemas, food or drink until you have examined him.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, consent, chaperone; lie him flat with one pillow, expose from chest to hips, keep the groin covered until needed.","Look from the end of the bed, then hands, face, chest, abdomen, groins; ask about pain first and watch his face as you press.","Offer a rectal examination with a chaperone and his consent."],"ask":["'When did you last open your bowels? Are you passing wind?'","'What did you vomit? Was it dark or smelly?'","'Where is the pain? Is it constant or does it come in waves?'","'Any leakage of watery stool?' (overflow around impaction)","'Have you had operations on your tummy?'","'How much do you drink over a week? Any yellow eyes or swollen legs?'","To the nurse: 'Is there a bowel chart? What laxatives is he on, and is he taking them?'"],"recognise":["Obstruction or ileus: distension, vomiting (especially faeculent), no flatus, tinkling or absent bowel sounds, tympanic percussion.","Peritonism (guarding, rebound, rigidity), fever, tachycardia or low BP suggest perforation or ischaemia: emergency.","Overflow diarrhoea can hide severe impaction; 'he's had loose stools' is not reassurance.","Hyoscine, other anticholinergics, opioids and dehydration all add to clozapine's effect on the gut.","Chronic liver disease: palmar erythema, Dupuytren's, leukonychia, spider naevi, gynaecomastia, jaundice, hepatomegaly, ascites (shifting dullness).","Hepatic encephalopathy: flapping tremor (asterixis), confusion, drowsiness, sweet fetor."],"decide":[{"c":"Stop food, laxatives and enemas; hold clozapine and hyoscine; urgent bloods; transfer to A&E for surgical review today with a written summary.","r":"Correct: distension, vomiting and tachycardia on clozapine point to obstruction or ileus that needs imaging and a surgeon.","best":true},{"c":"Give a phosphate enema and senna and review tomorrow.","r":"Dangerous: stimulants and enemas can perforate an obstructed bowel; this delays the surgical review he needs.","best":false},{"c":"Add lactulose and increase fluids, continue clozapine.","r":"Too slow for this picture; lactulose also worsens bloating. Prevention is right for later, not for possible obstruction now.","best":false}],"law":["Abdominal and rectal examination need his consent; record consent and the chaperone's name.","If he refuses transfer, assess capacity for that decision (MCA 2005); the MHA does not authorise treatment of bowel obstruction.","Detained patients need Section 17 leave and an escort to go to the acute hospital."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Your tummy is swollen and your bowel may have stopped moving, which can happen with clozapine. It needs a surgeon to look today. I'll write to them and a nurse will come with you."},{"to":"Surgical registrar","s":"47-year-old man on clozapine 450 mg and hyoscine, no bowel movement for 12 days, vomiting, distended tympanic abdomen with tinkling bowel sounds, HR 104. I'm worried about clozapine-induced obstruction or ileus and am sending him to A&E now."}],"write":"11:50 Reviewed re constipation. 47M, clozapine 450 mg/day, hyoscine 300 mcg tds.\nS: No BO 12 days, no flatus 2 days, vomited x2 (bilious), colicky pain. Drinks ~50 units/week on leave.\nO: HR 104, BP 112/70, T 37.6, RR 18. Distended, tympanic, mild generalised tenderness, no guarding. Tinkling bowel sounds. No hernia. No liver stigmata, no flap. PR (chaperone RN Patel): empty rectum.\nA: Suspected clozapine-induced bowel obstruction or ileus.\nP: NBM. No laxatives or enemas. Clozapine and hyoscine held. FBC, U&E, LFT, CRP, lactate, amylase, clozapine level. Transfer to A&E for surgical review with escort. RC informed.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["General: distress, jaundice, sweating, dehydration, distension visible from the end of the bed.","Hands: flapping tremor (arms out, wrists cocked back for 15 s), palmar erythema, Dupuytren's, leukonychia, clubbing.","Face and chest: scleral icterus, pallor, spider naevi, gynaecomastia.","Abdomen: scars, distension; light then deep palpation of all nine regions watching his face; liver and spleen, starting in the right iliac fossa up; ballot kidneys.","Percuss: tympanic distension, liver span, shifting dullness for ascites. Listen for bowel sounds for at least 30 s; tinkling or silence is abnormal.","Hernial orifices; offer rectal examination for impaction or empty rectum, with chaperone.","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, CRP, lactate (venous gas), amylase, clozapine level; abdominal imaging at the acute hospital."],"next":"CT at the acute hospital shows a dilated colon without perforation. He is managed conservatively. Before he returns, you plan a bowel regimen, stop hyoscine and agree a weekly bowel check with the care team.","marks":["Treated 12 days without a bowel movement on clozapine as a possible emergency.","Systematic abdominal examination including hernias and an offer of rectal examination.","Looked for liver disease signs given his drinking.","Stopped enemas and stimulants until obstruction was excluded.","Held clozapine and anticholinergics and planned prevention for return."],"pitfalls":["Giving an enema before examining.","Missing overflow diarrhoea.","Leaving hyoscine on the chart after the event.","Using bulk-forming laxatives on clozapine; they can worsen a slow gut."],"refs":["1. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine, reminder of potentially fatal risk of intestinal obstruction, faecal impaction and paralytic ileus (Sep 2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. Clozapine summary of product characteristics (electronic Medicines Compendium; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: diagnosis and management of physical complications (2010, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: laxatives, treatment summary (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK mental health wards keep bowel charts for clozapine patients. Read them. Patients rarely volunteer constipation, so ask at every review.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Examined the abdomen in full, including hernial orifices, liver signs and an offered rectal examination","Named obstruction or ileus as the working diagnosis and listed the signs that support it","Stopped laxatives, enemas and oral intake, and held clozapine and hyoscine","Arranged same-day surgical review with a written summary and an escort","Written a bowel prevention plan for his return"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just constipation. I always get it. Give me something and I'll be fine.\"","r":"\"You've been very patient with it. Twelve days, with vomiting and a swollen tummy, is more than usual constipation on clozapine. I need a surgeon to check today.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are they going to cut me open?\"","r":"\"Most people with this get better with rest for the bowel, a drip and close watching. The surgeons decide after a scan. I'll tell them everything so you don't have to repeat it.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to that hospital. Last time I waited 12 hours.\"","r":"\"That sounds miserable, and I'm sorry. This time I'm phoning ahead and writing a letter, and a nurse will go with you. Waiting here could make this much more dangerous.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I stopped taking the laxatives. They made me run to the toilet.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest. That's common, and it's not your fault. When you're back, we'll find a regimen that works without rushing you to the toilet.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why does clozapine do this?\"","r":"\"It slows the muscles in the gut wall, so things move very slowly. It's common and can be serious, which is why we ask about your bowels every week.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Now: nil by mouth, IV fluids at the acute hospital; hold clozapine and hyoscine; levels and bloods as above","On return: senna 15 mg at night plus docusate 200 mg twice daily; add macrogol 1 to 2 sachets daily if no bowel movement for 2 days","Avoid bulk-forming laxatives; stop hyoscine; agree another approach to drooling with the pharmacist","If clozapine is held more than 48 h, re-titrate from 12.5 mg with the pharmacist","Alcohol: AUDIT, LFT, thiamine 100 mg three times a day if drinking heavily"],"psych":["Explain why bowel checks matter so he reports early; agree a phrase he is comfortable using","Monitor mental state while clozapine is held; relapse can follow within days"],"social":["Care coordinator to add bowel monitoring to his care plan for leave and discharge","Supported accommodation staff (if any) given the bowel plan and red flags in writing"],"safety":["Bowel chart daily; nurse to escalate no bowel movement for 3 days, vomiting or distension","Weekly clozapine review includes bowel habit","RC informed; incident report if harm"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clozapine constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows the whole gut. Obstruction, impaction and ileus have caused deaths in the UK. Ask about bowels at every review; patients rarely raise it."},{"h":"Prevent from day one","x":"Start a softener plus stimulant, such as docusate and senna, when clozapine starts. Avoid bulk-forming laxatives in a slow gut."},{"h":"No enema before examination","x":"Distension, vomiting, no flatus, tinkling or absent bowel sounds: stop laxatives and enemas, keep him nil by mouth, and get a surgical review."},{"h":"Overflow fools people","x":"Watery leakage around a hard faecal mass looks like diarrhoea. Examine before you treat 'loose stools' in anyone on clozapine."},{"h":"Anticholinergics add up","x":"Hyoscine for drooling, procyclidine, tricyclics and opioids all slow the gut further. Review each one after an event."}]},
{"id":"c-g-04","n":904,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🛏","title":"Swollen neck in a woman on lithium: examine the thyroid","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:00, ward round follow-up","where":"Female acute ward, adult inpatient unit","text":"A 52-year-old woman with bipolar disorder, on lithium carbonate 800 mg at night for nine years, was admitted with low mood. She mentions her neck 'looks fatter' in photos, she has gained 4 kg and feels cold and slow. Her last thyroid test was 14 months ago."},"task":"Take a brief thyroid history, examine the thyroid and the signs of thyroid dysfunction, interpret the results and plan treatment with lithium continued.","first60":["Check the lithium monitoring record: last level, TFT, U&E, eGFR and calcium, and their dates.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, consent, chaperone offer; sit her on a chair with room to stand behind her, neck exposed to the collarbones, a glass of water ready.","Look from the front, then ask her to sip and hold, then swallow; then stick out her tongue.","Palpate from behind, then nodes, then percuss and listen; then hands, pulse, eyes and reflexes.","Send TFT, calcium, U&E, eGFR and a 12-hour lithium level today."],"ask":["'When did you first notice the swelling? Is it growing?'","'Any pain in the neck, change in your voice, trouble swallowing or breathing?'","'Do you feel the cold more than others? Any change in your skin, hair or periods?'","'Any constipation, aches, pins and needles in your hands?'","'Have you been very thirsty or passing lots of urine?'","'Any thyroid problems in the family? Any radiation to the neck in the past?'","'Is your mood low in a way that feels different from your usual depressions?'"],"recognise":["Lithium causes goitre and hypothyroidism, more often in women and in the first years; also raises calcium through the parathyroids.","Goitre moves up on swallowing; a thyroglossal cyst moves up when the tongue is protruded.","Diffuse, smooth, non-tender enlargement fits lithium or autoimmune thyroiditis; a single hard nodule, fixed mass, nodes or hoarseness need cancer referral.","Hypothyroid signs: slow pulse, dry skin, puffy face, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes, carpal tunnel, weight gain.","Hyperthyroid signs (rarer with lithium): fine tremor, warm sweaty hands, fast or irregular pulse, lid lag; eye protrusion points to Graves'.","Lithium tremor is fine and present on holding the arms out; a coarse tremor suggests toxicity.","Hypothyroidism can deepen depression and drive rapid cycling: treat it as part of the mood plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Continue lithium, confirm with TFT, start levothyroxine if hypothyroid, check calcium and PTH, and refer for ultrasound only if there is a nodule or a red flag.","r":"Correct: lithium-induced hypothyroidism is treated, not a reason to stop a drug that keeps her well.","best":true},{"c":"Stop lithium today because it has damaged her thyroid.","r":"Wrong: stopping lithium suddenly risks relapse, especially mania, and the hypothyroidism is easily treated.","best":false},{"c":"Note the neck swelling for the GP to look at after discharge.","r":"Delays treatment of a cause of her low mood and misses the lithium monitoring gap.","best":false}],"law":["Examination and blood tests need her consent; she has capacity and agrees.","Lithium monitoring is a shared responsibility; whoever holds the prescription must make sure the tests are done and acted on.","NICE NG12: an unexplained thyroid lump warrants a suspected cancer pathway referral."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like to examine your neck. I'll look first, ask you to swallow some water, then feel it from behind you. Please tell me if anything is tender."},{"to":"Patient (results)","s":"Your thyroid is underactive, which lithium can cause. It's common and very treatable with a daily tablet. You can carry on with lithium, and treating this may help your mood and energy too."},{"to":"GP (discharge letter)","s":"Lithium-induced hypothyroidism found on admission. Started levothyroxine 100 micrograms each morning. Please check TSH every 3 months until stable, then yearly; lithium monitoring due as listed."}],"write":"10:20 Neck swelling on lithium. 52F, Li 800 mg nocte x9 yrs.\nS: Neck swelling ~6 months, cold, weight +4 kg, slow. No hoarseness, dysphagia or stridor. Last TFT 14 months ago.\nO: Pulse 58 reg. Diffuse smooth goitre, moves with swallowing, not with tongue protrusion, non-tender, no nodules, no nodes, no bruit, no retrosternal dullness. Dry skin, slow-relaxing ankle jerks. Fine postural tremor. No eye signs.\nA: Probable lithium-induced hypothyroidism with diffuse goitre.\nP: TFT, calcium, PTH if Ca raised, U&E, eGFR, 12-h Li level. Continue lithium. Start levothyroxine if confirmed. Monitoring schedule restarted.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Inspect from the front with the neck slightly extended: swelling, scars, redness.","Sip water, hold, swallow: watch the swelling rise. Ask her to stick out her tongue: watch again.","From behind, with her chin slightly down: palpate both lobes and the isthmus with fingertips; repeat while she swallows; note size, texture, tenderness, nodules.","Feel cervical and supraclavicular nodes; percuss the upper sternum for retrosternal extension; listen for a bruit over each lobe.","Hands: tremor with arms out, temperature, sweating; radial pulse rate and rhythm.","Eyes: lid retraction, lid lag (follow your finger down), protrusion from behind and above, eye movements.","Ankle reflexes for slow relaxation; ask her to stand from the chair with arms folded (proximal weakness).","Bloods: TSH, free T4, calcium, U&E, eGFR, lithium level 12 hours after the last dose."],"next":"TSH 14 mU/L, free T4 low, calcium 2.68 mmol/L. You start levothyroxine, request PTH and tell her consultant. Her lithium level is 0.7 mmol/L. Three weeks later her energy is improving.","marks":["Checked the lithium monitoring record first.","Thyroid examination in the right order: inspect, swallow, tongue, palpate from behind, nodes, percuss, listen.","Looked for systemic signs: pulse, hands, eyes, reflexes.","Continued lithium and treated the hypothyroidism.","Checked calcium and knew when to refer for a lump."],"pitfalls":["Stopping lithium for hypothyroidism.","Forgetting calcium and the parathyroids.","Reading a lithium tremor as a thyroid sign without checking the level.","Missing hypothyroidism as a driver of depression."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG145 Thyroid disease: assessment and management (2019; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG12 Suspected cancer: recognition and referral (2015, updated; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: lithium carbonate; levothyroxine sodium (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Underactive thyroid (hypothyroidism) (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, lithium is usually monitored under a shared care agreement between psychiatry and the GP. On admission, check who did the last tests and what is overdue; gaps are common.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Examined the thyroid including swallow, tongue protrusion, nodes, retrosternal percussion and a bruit","Looked for and recorded pulse, tremor, eye signs and ankle reflexes","Sent TSH, free T4, calcium, U&E, eGFR and a 12-hour lithium level","Explained lithium-induced hypothyroidism and that lithium can continue","Written a monitoring plan with dates for TSH and lithium bloods"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it cancer? My aunt had a lump in her neck.\"","r":"\"I can understand that worry. The swelling feels smooth and even on both sides, which fits lithium rather than cancer. A blood test will confirm it, and if anything changes we'll scan it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody told me lithium could do this. Why wasn't it checked?\"","r":"\"You're right to be annoyed; the test was overdue. I'm sorry. I've sent it today, and I'll make sure the dates for your next checks are written down for you and your GP.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop lithium now?\"","r":"\"No. Lithium has kept you well for years. An underactive thyroid is treated with a daily tablet, and you carry on with lithium. We just check both more closely.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just eat more seaweed or take a supplement instead?\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd want something natural. Extra iodine won't fix this and can sometimes make it worse. The hormone tablet replaces exactly what your thyroid isn't making.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been feeling worse than my usual depressions. Heavier, slower.\"","r":"\"That's really helpful. An underactive thyroid can do exactly that. Treating it may lift some of the heaviness, alongside your other treatment.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Levothyroxine about 1.6 micrograms/kg daily (e.g. 100 micrograms at 65 kg), on an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast; 25 to 50 micrograms if over 65 or heart disease","TSH every 3 months after starting or any dose change until stable, then yearly","Continue lithium; aim for a level of 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L taken 12 hours after the dose","Lithium bloods: level every 3 months in year one then every 6 months; TFT, U&E, eGFR and calcium every 6 months","Calcium raised: send PTH; refer to endocrinology if hyperparathyroidism"],"psych":["Review the depression once thyroid is treated; avoid adding drugs for symptoms that may be thyroid-driven","Repeat PHQ-9 or a structured mood rating in 4 weeks to track change"],"social":["Give her a written lithium record (levels and test dates); GP and community team copied into the monitoring plan","Discharge letter to the GP lists levothyroxine dose and the next TSH and lithium test dates"],"safety":["Ultrasound and suspected cancer referral if a nodule, hard or fixed lump, nodes, hoarseness or rapid growth","Warn about lithium toxicity signs: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion: stop and get a level that day"]},"facts":[{"h":"Swallow lifts a goitre","x":"Thyroid swellings rise on swallowing; a thyroglossal cyst rises on tongue protrusion. Lymph nodes do neither."},{"h":"Treat the thyroid, keep the lithium","x":"Lithium-induced hypothyroidism is managed with levothyroxine. Stopping lithium risks relapse, especially mania, within weeks."},{"h":"Levothyroxine before breakfast","x":"Take on an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before food or caffeine. Separate from iron and calcium supplements by at least 4 hours."},{"h":"Lithium raises calcium too","x":"Lithium can cause hyperparathyroidism. Check calcium every 6 months; if raised, send PTH."},{"h":"Thyroid can drive low mood","x":"Hypothyroidism mimics and worsens depression and is linked to rapid cycling. Check TFT before changing mood treatment."}]},
{"id":"c-g-05","n":905,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Sudden loss of vision in one eye, \"probably psychological\": look at the fundi","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:00","where":"Acute medical ward, district general hospital (liaison psychiatry referral)","text":"The medical team refer a 31-year-old woman admitted yesterday with loss of vision in her left eye that came on over a morning. The referral says 'recent job loss and breakup, likely functional'. It does not say whether anyone has looked at her fundi or pupils."},"task":"Take a focused visual history, check acuity, colour, pupils, fields and the fundi, and decide whether this is an eye or brain emergency before any functional label; then screen mood and risk.","first60":["Read the notes: obs, BP, bloods, any neurology or ophthalmology input, any imaging.","Borrow an ophthalmoscope and a pen torch; check the ophthalmoscope light works and set the lens to 0.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, consent; explain each step before you do it.","Order: acuity, colour, pupils (including swinging light), fields, eye movements, then fundi in a dimmed room.","Do not dilate the pupils before checking pupil reactions, and only with the medical team's agreement."],"ask":["'Tell me exactly what happened to your sight. Sudden or over hours?'","'Is it blurred, dark, or a curtain over part of the vision? Which part?'","'Does it hurt to move your eye?'","'Are colours washed out in that eye, especially red?'","'Any flashing lights or new floaters?'","'Any headache, vomiting, weakness, numbness or tingling, now or in the past?'","'How have you been in yourself lately? Have you had felt that life isn't worth living?'"],"recognise":["Optic neuritis: young woman, vision loss over hours to days, pain on eye movement, red desaturation, relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), disc normal or swollen; may be the first sign of MS.","RAPD on the swinging light test means organic disease of the optic nerve or retina: it cannot be produced voluntarily.","Central retinal artery occlusion: sudden painless loss, pale retina, cherry-red spot; older or vascular patients; same-day emergency.","Retinal detachment: flashes, floaters, then a curtain; urgent ophthalmology.","Papilloedema: swollen discs in both eyes; think raised intracranial pressure, idiopathic intracranial hypertension or venous sinus thrombosis.","Functional vision loss is diagnosed on positive signs (normal pupils and fundi, inconsistent fields or acuity), not on stress alone; organic and functional problems can co-exist."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the eye checks; if RAPD, reduced colour vision or an abnormal disc, ask for same-day ophthalmology and neurology review and MRI; only discuss a functional cause once these are normal.","r":"Correct: the eye examination decides the pathway, and a missed optic neuritis or artery occlusion costs sight.","best":true},{"c":"Agree it is functional because of the recent stressors and offer psychology.","r":"Unsafe: life stress is common and proves nothing; this is how organic disease gets missed.","best":false},{"c":"Advise the team you cannot comment because you are not an eye doctor.","r":"Unhelpful: basic eye checks are within your skills, and you are the first doctor to look.","best":false}],"law":["Examination needs her consent; she has capacity.","Confidentiality: discuss stressors with the medical team only as far as relevant to her care.","DVLA: she should not drive until her vision is assessed; visual standards apply to all licence holders."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm going to check your eyes properly: reading letters, colours, how your pupils react to light, your side vision, and then I'll look at the back of each eye with a light. It's bright but shouldn't hurt."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Left eye: acuity 6/36, red desaturation, left RAPD, pain on eye movement, disc looks normal. This looks like optic neuritis, not functional loss. Could we get same-day ophthalmology and neurology review and an MRI of brain and orbits?"}],"write":"14:30 Liaison review. 31F, L visual loss.\nS: L vision blurred and dim over a morning 2 days ago, ache on eye movement, colours washed out. No flashes, floaters or headache. 3 years ago: numb legs for 2 weeks, resolved.\nO: Acuity R 6/6, L 6/36 (pinhole no better). Red desaturation L. L RAPD. Central scotoma L. EOM full, pain on movement. Fundi: discs pink, sharp margins, no haemorrhages, macula normal bilaterally.\nMood: low since job loss, no hopelessness, fleeting passive thoughts, no plans; protective factors sister, cat.\nA: Probable L optic neuritis, possible demyelination. Not functional. Adjustment-related low mood, low risk.\nP: Advised same-day ophthalmology and neurology, MRI brain and orbits. Not to drive. Liaison follow-up for mood.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Acuity: Snellen at 6 m (or a near card), each eye separately, with glasses or a pinhole.","Colour: Ishihara plates each eye, or ask her to compare the redness of a red object between eyes.","Pupils: direct and consensual; swinging light, 2 to 3 seconds each side: a pupil that dilates when the light reaches it is an RAPD; then accommodation.","Fields: sit 1 m apart, cover opposite eyes, bring a finger or red pin in from each quadrant; check for a central scotoma.","Eye movements in an H; ask about pain and double vision.","Fundoscopy: dim room, she fixes on a distant point; your right eye for her right eye; red reflex from arm's length; come in about 15 degrees from the side to find the disc; check disc colour, margins and cup; follow vessels to four quadrants; ask her to look at the light briefly for the macula.","Record each finding for each eye separately."],"next":"Ophthalmology confirm left optic neuritis. MRI shows white matter lesions; neurology take over. She is frightened about MS, and you arrange liaison follow-up to support her through the diagnosis.","marks":["Did not accept the functional label without examining.","Checked acuity, colour, pupils with swinging light, fields and fundi in each eye.","Recognised RAPD as an organic sign.","Asked about past neurological symptoms.","Screened mood and suicide risk without letting it drive the diagnosis.","Gave a clear, specific recommendation to the medical team."],"pitfalls":["Treating stress as evidence of a functional cause.","Dilating pupils before checking reactions.","Looking only at the disc and missing the macula and vessels.","Forgetting to advise about driving."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Ophthalmologists: guidance on urgent referral for acute visual loss (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG220 Multiple sclerosis in adults: management (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, a guide for medical professionals, visual disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Optic neuritis (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: liaison referrals in UK hospitals sometimes arrive with a psychological label already attached. Your job is to check the basics with fresh eyes; a clear, polite note of an organic sign is welcomed.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Recorded acuity, colour vision, pupil reactions including the swinging light test, fields and fundi for each eye","Stated whether an RAPD is present and what it means","Recommended a specific same-day review (ophthalmology, neurology, imaging) when signs are organic","Screened mood and suicide risk and recorded protective factors","Advised her not to drive until her vision is assessed"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"They think I'm making it up. Why else would they send a psychiatrist?\"","r":"\"That must feel awful. I'm not here because anyone thinks you're pretending. I want to check your eyes carefully myself, and I'll tell you honestly what I find.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can stress really make you go blind?\"","r":"\"Stress can affect the body in real ways, and sometimes vision. But I don't assume that. First I check the eye and the nerve, because those findings guide what we do.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it a brain tumour?\"","r":"\"I can hear how scared you are. What I found points more to inflammation of the eye nerve, which often improves. A scan will help us be sure, and I'll stay involved.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"A few years ago my legs went numb for a fortnight. I never told anyone.\"","r":"\"Thank you. That's important, and I'll tell the neurology team. Two episodes affecting different nerves is something they'll want to look at closely.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"First my job, then him, now this. I can't cope with any more.\"","r":"\"That's a huge amount at once, and it makes sense you feel overwhelmed. Can I ask how low things have got? Have you had thoughts about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you're saying it is all in my head, then?\"","r":"\"No. Your pupil reaction shows a real problem with the nerve in that eye. That's a physical sign, and it needs the eye and nerve specialists today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day ophthalmology review; neurology review and MRI brain and orbits if optic neuritis","Bloods per medical team: FBC, CRP, ESR, glucose; BP and urine if retinal vascular signs","Any steroid decision rests with neurology or ophthalmology, not liaison"],"psych":["Liaison follow-up this week: adjustment, health anxiety about MS, mood","If a functional component is later confirmed, explain it as a real, common problem and refer to neuro-ophthalmology or functional neurology services"],"social":["Fit note from the medical team; ward social worker or Citizens Advice for benefits after job loss","With consent, involve her sister for support at diagnosis and after discharge"],"safety":["Low suicide risk recorded with protective factors; give crisis line number (NHS 111, option 2)","Not to drive until cleared; documented","Liaison review again before discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"RAPD cannot be faked","x":"A relative afferent pupillary defect on the swinging light test means optic nerve or retinal disease. Its presence ends any 'purely functional' discussion."},{"h":"Pupils before drops","x":"Check pupil reactions and record them before anyone dilates the eyes. Dilated pupils hide an RAPD and block neurological obs."},{"h":"Pain on moving the eye","x":"Vision loss over hours to days with pain on eye movement and washed-out red in a young woman: think optic neuritis."},{"h":"Stress is not a diagnosis","x":"Recent stress is common in everyone admitted to hospital. Functional vision loss is diagnosed on positive examination signs, never on life events alone."},{"h":"Right eye with right eye","x":"Hold the ophthalmoscope in your right hand to your right eye for her right eye, and vice versa, so your noses do not meet."}]},
{"id":"c-g-06","n":906,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🛏","title":"Numb, weak legs in a man admitted with depression: the limb neurological examination","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:00","where":"Male acute ward, adult inpatient unit","text":"A 27-year-old man admitted five days ago with depression tells the nurse his feet and fingers have been tingling for three weeks and he keeps stumbling, worse in the dark. He used large nitrous oxide canisters most weekends for a year. Nursing staff wonder if it's anxiety."},"task":"Do a structured upper and lower limb neurological examination, map the findings to a level or a pattern, and decide on bloods, imaging and neurology referral.","first60":["Ask: 'Any back pain, bladder or bowel change, numbness around the bottom?' Saddle numbness or retention means same-day emergency imaging.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, consent, chaperone; shorts and vest, lying on the bed; tendon hammer, 128 Hz tuning fork, neuro-tip.","Watch him walk first, then Romberg; this often tells you the answer.","Then lower limbs and upper limbs in the same order: look, tone, power, reflexes, coordination, sensation.","Compare left with right at every step."],"ask":["'Where exactly is the tingling? Did it start in the feet or the hands?'","'Is it worse in the dark or with your eyes closed?'","'Any weakness, falls, or trouble with buttons or stairs?'","'Any back or neck pain? Any change with your bladder or bowels?'","'How many canisters, how often, and when was the last time?'","'What do you eat in a normal day? Any alcohol?'","'Any recent tummy upset or flu before this started?' (Guillain-Barré)"],"recognise":["Nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B12: subacute combined degeneration with dorsal column loss (vibration, joint position), positive Romberg, sensory ataxia.","Mixed upper and lower motor neurone signs (absent ankle jerks with up-going plantars) fit B12 deficiency.","Cord compression: sensory level on the trunk, spastic weakness, brisk reflexes, up-going plantars, bladder changes: emergency MRI.","Cauda equina: back pain, saddle numbness, urinary retention or incontinence, reduced ankle jerks: emergency MRI.","Guillain-Barré: rapidly ascending weakness, lost reflexes, after infection; check breathing (FVC) urgently.","Functional weakness: positive signs such as Hoover's sign and give-way weakness; never a reason to skip the full examination.","Myotomes and reflexes: knee L3 to L4, ankle S1, biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7; foot dorsiflexion L4 to L5, plantarflexion S1."],"decide":[{"c":"Examine fully; send B12, folate, FBC, homocysteine or methylmalonic acid; give IM hydroxocobalamin without waiting for results; refer urgently to neurology with MRI spine; emergency imaging the same day if bladder or saddle signs.","r":"Correct: early B12 replacement changes the outcome, and you have screened for compression.","best":true},{"c":"Record 'tingling, probably anxiety-related hyperventilation' and review next week.","r":"Unsafe: his gait and history point to a treatable cord problem; delay risks permanent damage.","best":false},{"c":"Start oral vitamin B12 tablets and refer routinely to the GP.","r":"Too little, too slow: neurological B12 deficiency needs IM treatment and specialist review.","best":false}],"law":["Possession of nitrous oxide for its psychoactive effect is an offence (Class C, since November 2023); your role is health, not reporting him.","Examination and injections need his consent; he has capacity.","If detained, treatment for this physical problem still needs consent or the MCA 2005."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like to check the nerves in your arms and legs: how you walk, the strength in your muscles, your reflexes and what you can feel. It takes a few minutes and I'll explain as I go."},{"to":"Neurology registrar","s":"27-year-old man with a year of heavy nitrous oxide use. Three weeks of tingling feet and hands, positive Romberg, loss of vibration and joint position to the knees, absent ankle jerks, up-going plantars. Suspected subacute combined degeneration. B12 sent, hydroxocobalamin started. Could he be seen this week with an MRI spine?"}],"write":"15:30 Neuro review. 27M, depression, N2O use ~1 yr.\nS: 3 wks tingling feet then fingers, unsteady in dark. No back pain, sphincters normal, no saddle numbness.\nO: Broad-based gait, Romberg positive. LL: tone normal, power 5/5 except hip flexion 4/5 bilat. Knees brisk, ankles absent, plantars up-going bilat. Vibration absent to knees, JPS impaired at toes. Light touch reduced to mid-shin. No sensory level on trunk. UL: vibration reduced at fingers, reflexes normal, finger-nose normal eyes open.\nA: Probable subacute combined degeneration from N2O-induced B12 deficiency.\nP: B12, folate, FBC, homocysteine/MMA, U&E. Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM today. Urgent neurology, MRI spine. Stop N2O. Falls plan.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Gait: normal walk, heel-to-toe; Romberg (feet together, eyes open then closed: falls only with eyes closed = sensory ataxia).","Look: wasting, fasciculation, scars, foot shape. Tone: roll the legs, lift the knee briskly, test ankle clonus (more than 3 beats is abnormal).","Power (grade 0 to 5): hip and knee flexion and extension, ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion, big toe extension.","Reflexes: knee, ankle, plantars; use reinforcement (clench teeth) before calling one absent.","Coordination: heel-shin. Sensation: light touch and pin by dermatome, vibration from the big toe up, joint position at the big toe with eyes closed; check for a level on the trunk.","Upper limbs: arms out, palms up, eyes closed (drift); tone; power at shoulder, elbow, wrist, fingers; biceps, supinator, triceps reflexes; finger-nose; sensation as above.","Bladder scan after voiding if any urinary symptoms; bloods: B12, folate, FBC, homocysteine or MMA, U&E, glucose."],"next":"B12 is low-normal but homocysteine is high, confirming functional deficiency. MRI shows dorsal column change. Neurology continue hydroxocobalamin and plan physiotherapy. You add nitrous oxide use to his formulation and arrange drug service input.","marks":["Screened for cauda equina and cord compression first.","Examined gait and Romberg before lying him down.","Followed a fixed order and compared sides.","Mapped findings to a pattern (dorsal columns, mixed UMN and LMN).","Started IM B12 promptly and referred urgently.","Took a non-judgemental drug history."],"pitfalls":["Labelling sensory symptoms as anxiety without examining.","Calling an ankle jerk absent without reinforcement.","Relying on a normal serum B12 in nitrous oxide use.","Giving folic acid alone before B12 replacement."],"refs":["1. NICE NG239 Vitamin B12 deficiency in over 16s: diagnosis and management (2024; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG234 Spinal metastases and metastatic spinal cord compression (2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: hydroxocobalamin (checked Nov 2026)","4. Home Office: nitrous oxide controlled as a Class C drug (Nov 2023; checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Cauda equina syndrome (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: many UK psychiatric wards have no neurology on site. A clear written examination with a named pattern gets your referral accepted quickly.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Screened for cauda equina and cord compression red flags and recorded the answers","Completed gait, Romberg, and a full upper and lower limb examination in a fixed order","Named the pattern of findings and the likely level or cause","Sent B12, folate, FBC and homocysteine or MMA and started IM hydroxocobalamin","Made an urgent neurology referral with MRI spine requested"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just laughing gas. It's harmless, everyone does it.\"","r":"\"Lots of people think that. Heavy use blocks vitamin B12, which the spinal cord needs, and your examination shows signs of that. The good news is that treatment started early can help.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to end up in a wheelchair?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought. Many people improve a lot with B12 injections and stopping the gas, especially when caught early, as yours has been. We'll get the neurologists involved this week.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you want me to close my eyes and stand still?\"","r":"\"It tests the nerves that tell your brain where your feet are. If they're not working, you rely on your eyes, so you sway when they close. I'll stand right next to you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I was using it every day by the end. It was the only thing that helped my mood.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me straight. That helps us understand your depression and your nerves. Let's treat both, and find you something that helps without these effects.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I hate needles. Can't I just take a tablet?\"","r":"\"I understand. When nerves are affected, injections work more reliably at first. It's a small needle in the arm, and the nurse can use numbing cream. Tablets may be possible later.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM on alternate days until no further improvement, then every 2 months (BNF regimen for neurological involvement); neurology may adjust","Do not give folic acid alone before B12 is replaced","Bloods: B12, folate, FBC, homocysteine or MMA before the first injection if possible; repeat FBC in 10 days","MRI spine via neurology; emergency MRI the same day if saddle numbness, retention or a sensory level appears"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing about nitrous oxide; link use to mood and coping in the formulation","Continue depression treatment; explain that low B12 can worsen mood and thinking"],"social":["Community drug and alcohol service referral before discharge","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy assessment for gait and home safety"],"safety":["Falls risk assessment; supervised showering; night light","Nurses to report new bladder symptoms, back pain or worsening weakness the same shift","Neurology review date recorded in notes"]},"facts":[{"h":"Gas switches off B12","x":"Nitrous oxide inactivates B12, so serum B12 can look normal. A raised homocysteine or methylmalonic acid confirms functional deficiency."},{"h":"Romberg tests the dorsal columns","x":"Steady with eyes open, falls with eyes closed: loss of joint position sense. Stand close, arms ready, before asking him to close his eyes."},{"h":"B12 before folate","x":"Giving folic acid alone in B12 deficiency can worsen the spinal cord damage. Start hydroxocobalamin first."},{"h":"Saddle numbness is an emergency","x":"Back pain with saddle numbness, new retention or incontinence needs emergency MRI the same day for cauda equina."},{"h":"Absent ankles, up-going toes","x":"Combined UMN and LMN signs in the legs point to B12 deficiency, among a short list. Write the pattern, not just the findings."}]},
{"id":"c-g-07","n":907,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🛏","title":"Brief spells of feeling unreal with odd mouth movements: could this be epilepsy?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:00","where":"Female acute ward, adult inpatient unit","text":"A 23-year-old woman was admitted with 'dissociative episodes and anxiety'. A nurse saw one this morning: she stared, smacked her lips and picked at her sleeve for about a minute, did not answer, then was muddled for ten minutes. She says each spell starts with a rising feeling in her stomach and 'I've been here before'. She drives to work."},"task":"Take a focused history of the episodes from her and the witness, explain that this may be focal epilepsy, and arrange ECG, neurology review, EEG and MRI, with safety and driving advice.","first60":["Speak to the nurse who saw it while it is fresh: what exactly happened, from start to finish, and how long?","Ask staff to film the next episode on a ward device if she agrees, and to record each one on a seizure chart.","Check her medication chart for drugs that lower the seizure threshold.","Do a 12-lead ECG today: blackouts need a cardiac cause excluded.","Do a neurological examination and check for tongue bite or injury."],"ask":["'What is the very first thing you notice when a spell starts?'","'Do you know what is happening around you during it? Can you hear people?'","'How long do they last? How do you feel afterwards?'","'How often? Are they getting more frequent? Any at night?'","'Have you ever wet yourself, bitten your tongue or woken up on the floor?'","'Any head injury, meningitis, fits with fevers as a child, or epilepsy in the family?'","'Do you drive? Have you had any spells while driving?'","To the nurse: 'Were her eyes open or closed? Did she respond to her name? Did her movements stop and start?'"],"recognise":["Focal seizures from the temporal lobe: rising stomach sensation, déjà vu, fear, smells or tastes, then staring and automatisms (lip smacking, chewing, picking), lasting 30 seconds to 2 minutes, followed by confusion.","Stereotyped: each episode looks much the same; often brief and can occur from sleep.","Dissociative (functional) seizures: often longer, eyes closed, fluctuating, side-to-side head movements, good recall; diagnosis needs specialist review and often video-EEG.","Panic attacks: build over minutes, awareness kept, breathlessness, no automatisms, no post-episode confusion.","Drugs that lower seizure threshold: clozapine, bupropion, high-dose tricyclics, alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal.","Temporal lobe epilepsy carries higher rates of depression, anxiety and psychosis; treat both."],"decide":[{"c":"Record a detailed witness account, ECG, bloods, neurological examination; refer to neurology (first seizure clinic) for review within two weeks with MRI and EEG; advise no driving and safety measures now.","r":"Correct: this fits focal seizures and needs specialist diagnosis; safety and driving advice cannot wait.","best":true},{"c":"Document as dissociative episodes and increase her PRN lorazepam.","r":"Premature: the features point to epilepsy, and benzodiazepines would mask episodes without a diagnosis.","best":false},{"c":"Start an antiseizure medicine yourself today.","r":"Not your call alone: the diagnosis and drug choice belong to the epilepsy specialist unless she has repeated or prolonged seizures needing urgent treatment.","best":false}],"law":["DVLA: she must stop driving and tell the DVLA; record your advice. If she keeps driving and refuses to tell them, GMC guidance allows you to inform the DVLA after telling her you will.","Video recording of episodes needs her consent and must follow trust information governance.","MHRA 2024: valproate must not be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there is no suitable alternative."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"The spells you describe, starting with a rising feeling and déjà vu, then losing touch for a minute, can be a type of epilepsy that starts in one part of the brain. It's common and often treatable. I'd like a specialist to see you soon, with a brain scan and a brain wave test."},{"to":"Patient (driving)","s":"Until a specialist has seen you, you mustn't drive. The law requires you to tell the DVLA. I know that's hard with work; we can write a letter to your employer if it helps."},{"to":"Neurology","s":"23-year-old woman with stereotyped spells: epigastric rising, déjà vu, then unresponsive staring with lip smacking and picking for a minute, post-episode confusion ten minutes, witnessed today. ECG normal. Suspected focal impaired awareness seizures. Please see in first seizure clinic; she has been told not to drive."}],"write":"11:40 Episodes review. 23F, admitted with anxiety.\nWitness (RN Okafor, 09:15): stared, lip smacking, picking at sleeve ~60 s, eyes open, unresponsive to name, confused ~10 min after.\nPatient: epigastric rising + déjà vu at onset, ~2/week for 4 months, amnesia for middle. Febrile convulsion age 2. Drives.\nO: Neurological examination normal. No tongue bite. ECG sinus 72, QTc 410.\nMeds: sertraline 100 mg, no threshold-lowering drugs.\nA: Probable focal impaired awareness seizures, temporal onset. Panic and dissociation less likely.\nP: First seizure clinic referral (2 wk), MRI brain epilepsy protocol, EEG. FBC, U&E, Ca, Mg, glucose, LFT. Seizure chart, film episodes with consent. No driving advised, told to inform DVLA. Showers only, no bath unsupervised.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Witness account in writing: onset, eyes open or closed, responsiveness, movements, duration, colour, recovery.","12-lead ECG (QTc, Brugada pattern, heart block), lying and standing BP.","Bloods: FBC, U&E, glucose, calcium, magnesium, LFT.","Neurological examination; look for tongue bite or injuries.","Referral to first seizure or epilepsy clinic; MRI brain is the preferred imaging; EEG supports but cannot rule out epilepsy.","Seizure chart on the ward; written safety advice; driving advice recorded."],"next":"Neurology see her within two weeks. EEG shows left temporal sharp waves and MRI shows left hippocampal sclerosis. They start lamotrigine with a slow titration and ask you to watch her mood as the dose rises.","marks":["Got a detailed, structured witness account.","Asked about the onset sensation and post-episode state.","Distinguished focal seizures from panic and dissociation on features.","Excluded cardiac causes with an ECG.","Gave driving and safety advice and recorded it.","Referred to the right specialist with a clear summary."],"pitfalls":["Labelling stereotyped episodes as dissociative because she is on a psychiatric ward.","Using a normal EEG to rule out epilepsy.","Forgetting driving advice.","Choosing valproate for a young woman."],"refs":["1. NICE NG217 Epilepsies in children, young people and adults (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, a guide for medical professionals, neurological disorders (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC: Confidentiality, patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHRA Drug Safety Update: valproate, new safety measures for patients under 55 (Jan 2024; checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Epilepsy, symptoms (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the patient, not the doctor, is legally responsible for telling the DVLA. Your duty is to advise clearly, record it, and act if they continue to drive against advice.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Recorded a witness account covering onset, eyes, responsiveness, movements, duration and recovery","Asked about aura, awareness, frequency, nocturnal episodes, injuries and risk factors","Done an ECG and neurological examination and checked her drug chart for threshold-lowering drugs","Referred to the first seizure clinic with MRI and EEG requested","Advised no driving, informing the DVLA and bath and height safety, and documented it"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How can it be epilepsy? I've never had a proper fit.\"","r":"\"Good question. Most people picture falling and shaking, but some seizures start in one small area and just change awareness for a minute. What you describe fits that kind. A specialist will confirm it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So I can't drive? I'll lose my job.\"","r":"\"That's a real blow, and I'm sorry. Driving during a spell could kill you or someone else. I can write to your employer, and many people return to driving once seizures are controlled.\""},{"tag":"Relief","p":"\"So I'm not going mad? I thought it was all in my head.\"","r":"\"No, you're not. These spells come from the brain's electrical activity, not from your imagination. You've also had anxiety, and we'll keep helping with that too.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I have to take tablets forever?\"","r":"\"I understand that worry. The specialist will talk you through options; many people take one medicine and become seizure-free. Some can stop after years without seizures, with advice.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having them film me. That's creepy.\"","r":"\"That's completely your choice. A short video helps the specialist a lot, but we can rely on careful notes instead. If you change your mind, only the medical team would see it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I had one at the wheel last month. I pulled over just in time.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. It's exactly why driving has to stop now. Let's talk about how to tell the DVLA today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Antiseizure medicine started by neurology: lamotrigine or levetiracetam are first choice for focal seizures","Lamotrigine: 25 mg daily for 2 weeks, 50 mg daily for 2 weeks, then up slowly to 100 to 200 mg daily; stop and seek help for any rash; combined pill lowers levels","Levetiracetam: can cause irritability, low mood and suicidal thoughts; monitor mood weekly","Keep sertraline; avoid bupropion and high-dose tricyclics; review any threshold-lowering drug"],"psych":["Explain the diagnosis in plain terms and give the Epilepsy Action or Epilepsy Society leaflet","Continue CBT for anxiety; screen for depression at each review"],"social":["Letter for employer about driving; Access to Work if her job needs travel","Epilepsy specialist nurse contact once diagnosed"],"safety":["Showers rather than baths; no swimming or heights alone; ward seizure chart","If a seizure lasts 5 minutes or longer: emergency treatment per ward protocol and call 999","Driving advice recorded; review at next ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Déjà vu and lip smacking","x":"Rising stomach feeling, déjà vu or fear, then staring with chewing or picking, then confusion: classic temporal lobe focal seizure."},{"h":"Normal EEG does not exclude","x":"A routine EEG is often normal between seizures. Use it to support a specialist diagnosis, never to rule epilepsy out."},{"h":"ECG for every blackout","x":"Do a 12-lead ECG for any unexplained episode of lost awareness; look at QTc and conduction."},{"h":"The patient tells the DVLA","x":"Advise her not to drive and to inform the DVLA, and record it. If she refuses and keeps driving, tell her, then inform the DVLA yourself."},{"h":"No valproate under 55","x":"Valproate is not started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there is no alternative and pregnancy prevention is in place for women."}]},
{"id":"c-g-08","n":908,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"BMI 14 on the medical ward: anorexia history and medical risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 13:30","where":"Acute medical ward, district general hospital (liaison psychiatry referral)","text":"The medical team refer a 20-year-old woman, a university student, admitted after fainting in a lecture. She weighs 39 kg at 1.67 m (BMI 14.0). HR 44, BP 86/54 lying, potassium 2.9, phosphate 0.8. She says, 'I just need to eat more. I can go home tomorrow.'"},"task":"Take an eating disorder history, assess medical and psychiatric risk with the MEED framework, and advise the medical team on safe refeeding, monitoring and the legal options if she refuses.","first60":["Read the obs chart, bloods (K, phosphate, magnesium, glucose, LFT) and ECG before you see her.","Check with the nurse: is she being weighed properly (gown, after voiding, same scales)? Any vomiting or exercising on the ward?","Sit at her level; say why you're here and that you're working with the medical team.","Do a sit up and squat stand (SUSS) test and a postural BP if safe.","Phone the eating disorder service early; they know her or can advise today."],"ask":["'Can you take me through what you eat and drink in a typical day?'","'How has your weight changed? What was it six months ago?'","'Do you ever make yourself sick, or use laxatives, diuretics or diet pills?'","'How much exercise do you do in a day?'","'How do you feel about your body and your weight right now?'","'When was your last period?'","'Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?'","'Who knows about this? Who is supporting you?'"],"recognise":["BMI under 13 is high risk and 13 to 15 moderate; rapid loss (more than 1 kg a week) adds risk at any BMI.","High-risk signs: heart rate under 40, low BP with dizziness or a postural drop, temperature under 35 °C, prolonged QTc, low potassium, phosphate, magnesium or glucose.","SUSS test: can she sit up from lying and stand from squatting without using her hands? Failure means significant muscle weakness.","Refeeding syndrome: falling phosphate, potassium and magnesium in the first days of feeding; can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, confusion and death.","Low potassium with a normal-looking diet history suggests vomiting or laxative use.","Anorexia nervosa has the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder, from both medical complications and suicide.","Ambivalence is typical: 'I'll just eat more' often sits alongside fear of weight gain."],"decide":[{"c":"Advise the team she is high risk: stay in hospital, refeeding with dietitian, electrolytes daily, thiamine, cardiac monitoring, supervised meals, eating disorder service input; assess capacity for discharge and consider the MHA if she tries to leave.","r":"Correct: her HR, BP and electrolytes make discharge unsafe; the MEED framework guides each step.","best":true},{"c":"Agree she can go home with a GP appointment because she is keen to eat more.","r":"Unsafe: bradycardia, hypotension and low potassium put her at risk of sudden death.","best":false},{"c":"Recommend very low-calorie feeding (5 kcal/kg) to avoid refeeding syndrome.","r":"Overly cautious: underfeeding prolongs medical risk. Current guidance starts higher with close electrolyte monitoring.","best":false}],"law":["If she wants to leave and lacks capacity for that decision because of the illness, the MCA 2005 can support short-term treatment; record the capacity assessment.","The MHA 1983 can be used for anorexia nervosa; feeding, including by nasogastric tube, counts as treatment for the mental disorder under Section 63.","Capacity in anorexia is often impaired in using and weighing information about food and weight, even when she understands the facts."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've told me you want to eat more, and I believe part of you does. Your heart is beating very slowly and your salts are low, which can be dangerous. I'd like you to stay while your body gets used to food again."},{"to":"Medical consultant","s":"This is severe anorexia nervosa with high medical risk: BMI 14, HR 44, BP 86/54, potassium 2.9, failed SUSS. I'd recommend refeeding with dietitian input, daily bloods for a week, thiamine, ECG monitoring, supervised meals and bathroom access after meals. The eating disorder team will see her tomorrow."}],"write":"14:15 Liaison review. 20F, student.\nS: Restricting to ~500 kcal/day for 9 months, weight 52 to 39 kg. Vomits once daily, walks 2 h/day. Amenorrhoea 6 months. Fears weight gain. Passive death wishes, no plans.\nO: BMI 14.0. HR 44, BP 86/54 lying, 74/48 standing (dizzy). T 35.4. SUSS sit-up 1/3, squat 1/3. K 2.9, PO4 0.8, Mg 0.68, glucose 3.6. ECG sinus brady, QTc 462.\nA: Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge features. High medical risk (MEED). Moderate suicide risk.\nCapacity to refuse admission: lacks (cannot weigh risk; 'my heart is fine').\nP: Remain in hospital. Refeeding per dietitian, electrolytes daily x7 days, thiamine and vitamin B co strong, K replacement, cardiac monitoring. Supervised meals, 1 h post-meal bathroom supervision. ED team review tomorrow. If attempts to leave: senior review, consider MHA.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Calculate BMI from an accurate weight; plot trend; note rate of loss.","Obs: HR, lying and standing BP, temperature; ECG with QTc.","SUSS test: sit up from lying without hands; squat and stand without hands; score each 0 to 3.","Bloods: FBC, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, glucose, LFT, CK; repeat daily for the first week of refeeding.","Medical team: refeeding plan with dietitian, thiamine before and during feeding, electrolyte replacement.","Write a MEED-based care plan: supervised meals, post-meal observation, activity limits, weight twice weekly.","Refer to the specialist eating disorder service today; tell the GP and, with consent, her family or university welfare team."],"next":"On day 3 her phosphate falls to 0.5 and she becomes breathless. The medical team replace phosphate intravenously and continue feeding with closer monitoring. She asks to leave on day 5; she lacks capacity to decide and is detained under Section 2 to remain on the ward with eating disorder team support.","marks":["Read the physical data before the interview.","Took a full eating disorder history including purging and exercise.","Used MEED risk markers including the SUSS test.","Gave clear, specific advice on refeeding and monitoring.","Assessed suicide risk.","Assessed capacity and knew how the MHA applies."],"pitfalls":["Being reassured by a normal-looking potassium or a confident patient.","Advising very low starting calories.","Weighing in clothes or after drinking.","Forgetting purging and suicide risk."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Psychiatrists CR233 Medical emergencies in eating disorders (MEED): guidance on recognition and management (2022, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG69 Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (2017, updated 2020; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CG32 Nutrition support for adults (2006, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015) and Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Anorexia nervosa (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK medical wards often look after patients with severe anorexia because specialist beds are scarce. Liaison psychiatry advises; the medical team leads physical care. Write your advice as a numbered list in the notes so it gets done.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Taken a history covering intake, weight trend, purging, exercise, body image, periods and suicidal thoughts","Recorded BMI, rate of loss, HR, lying and standing BP, temperature, SUSS score, QTc and electrolytes","Rated her medical risk using MEED markers and stated it clearly to the medical team","Written specific advice on refeeding, thiamine, daily bloods and supervised meals","Assessed capacity to refuse admission and stated the legal route if she tries to leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not anorexic. I just got busy with coursework and forgot to eat.\"","r":"\"University can be hugely pressured. Your weight has dropped a lot and your heart is now very slow. Whatever we call it, your body needs help. Can we talk about what eating is like for you?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll eat everything if you let me go home tomorrow.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you want to eat. The trouble is your heart and salts need watching while you start, because the first days of eating carry their own risks. Let's set a goal for the next week together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I eat what they give me I'll just balloon.\"","r":"\"That fear is real and really common. The plan is measured and the dietitian adjusts it. Your body will use most of it to repair your heart and muscles first.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I make myself sick after most meals. Nobody knows.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It explains your low potassium, which affects your heart. We'll help you through the time after meals so you're not on your own with it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You can't keep me here. I'm an adult.\"","r":"\"You're right that adults make their own choices, and I respect that. My job is to check whether this illness is making the decision for you. Right now I'm worried it is.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Mother on the phone) \"Should I make her eat when she comes home?\"","r":"\"You clearly want to help. For now the team will manage meals here. The eating disorder service will guide you on meals at home; forcing food alone tends to backfire.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Refeeding plan by dietitian; MEED suggests around 1400 to 2000 kcal a day for most adults rather than very low starts","Thiamine 200 to 300 mg daily orally plus vitamin B compound strong 1 to 2 tablets three times a day, starting before feeding; multivitamin","Phosphate, potassium, magnesium and glucose daily for the first week, then twice weekly; replace as needed","ECG daily while QTc prolonged or potassium low; avoid QTc-prolonging drugs"],"psych":["Eating disorder service review and motivational work on the ward","Later, one of NICE's options for adult anorexia: CBT-ED, MANTRA or SSCM, delivered by the eating disorder team"],"social":["With consent, involve family and the university welfare team; extenuating circumstances for coursework deadlines","Carers' assessment and family support from the eating disorder service"],"safety":["Supervised meals and 1 hour post-meal observation; restrict exercise","Suicide risk reviewed daily; nurses aware of passive death wishes","If she tries to leave: medical and psychiatric senior review, capacity assessment, MHA assessment if indicated"]},"facts":[{"h":"SUSS shows hidden weakness","x":"Sit up from lying and stand from squatting without using hands. Failure means serious muscle loss and higher medical risk, even if she looks well."},{"h":"Don't underfeed","x":"Very low starting calories prolong medical danger. MEED advises a higher start with daily electrolytes and phosphate replacement."},{"h":"Phosphate falls in days 2 to 5","x":"Refeeding syndrome usually shows within the first week. Check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily and replace early."},{"h":"Low potassium means purging","x":"Unexplained low potassium in restrictive eating suggests vomiting or laxatives. Ask directly; it changes cardiac risk."},{"h":"Feeding is treatment","x":"Under the MHA 1983, feeding for anorexia nervosa, including by nasogastric tube, can count as treatment for the mental disorder."}]},
{"id":"c-g-09","n":909,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🏥","title":"Ready for discharge but his wife has a court order: planning where an older man goes next","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 14:00","where":"Older adult mental health ward","text":"A 74-year-old man was admitted six weeks ago after police were called to a domestic incident in which he assaulted his wife. His mood and sleep have improved on mirtazapine. His family now tell the ward he has drunk heavily for years and been violent before; his wife has obtained a court order stopping him returning home. He has not yet been told. She did all the cooking, money and medication."},"task":"Tell him he cannot go home, explore his understanding, daily living skills and wishes, agree a discharge plan covering accommodation, alcohol, social care and safeguarding, and explain your reasoning.","first60":["Check the facts first: the order's terms (from the police or family's solicitor), any bail conditions, and what the family agreed you may share.","Read the OT kitchen assessment, cognitive test results, and nursing notes on alcohol and self-care.","Ask the ward social worker to join or to meet straight after.","Book a quiet room; have a nurse nearby; sit with a clear exit.","Start with what he understands about why he is in hospital."],"ask":["'What's your understanding of why you came into hospital?'","'How are you feeling now compared with when you came in?'","'Before you came in, who did the cooking, shopping, bills and tablets?'","'Could you manage those yourself if you had to? Which would be hardest?'","'How much were you drinking in a normal week?'","'How do you feel about your wife at the moment? Have you had worries about her?'","'If you can't go home, where would you want to be? What matters most to you?'","'What would worry you most about living somewhere new?'"],"recognise":["News of the order is a shock; expect denial, anger or grief, and allow time.","He relied on his wife for meals, money and medicines: he will need support, at least at first.","Possible cognitive impairment, including alcohol-related brain damage; repeat cognitive testing after six weeks' abstinence.","Jealous beliefs about his wife raise risk to her and to any future partner; record and share within the risk plan.","Alcohol relapse risk is high at discharge, especially if isolated.","Options: own tenancy with home care, sheltered or extra care housing, residential care; nursing care only if he needs nursing input."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the order honestly, assess needs with him, ask the social worker for a Care Act needs assessment and housing referral, plan alcohol support, share discharge details with police and his wife's support worker, and agree a preferred option with him.","r":"Correct: he is involved, his care needs are met, and his wife is protected.","best":true},{"c":"Discharge him to a hostel as he is medically fit and has no fixed address.","r":"Unsafe: ignores his care needs, relapse risk and the duty to plan discharge properly.","best":false},{"c":"Suggest he goes home if his son stays with him for a few days.","r":"Breaks a court order and puts his wife at risk; never plan around an order.","best":false}],"law":["Care Act 2014: the local authority must assess his care and support needs; he is likely to meet eligibility.","Homelessness Reduction Act 2017: hospitals have a duty to refer people who may be homeless to the local housing authority, with consent.","MCA 2005: presume he has capacity to decide where to live; assess only if there is reason to doubt it, and record the assessment.","Domestic Abuse Act 2021 and local safeguarding procedures: his wife is a victim; share information proportionately with police, MARAC or her IDVA to keep her safe.","A Health and welfare LPA finance can be discussed while he has capacity to make one."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I have some difficult news. Your wife has got a court order which means you can't go back to the house for now. I know this may be a shock. I'd like to understand how you feel, and then plan together where you'll live and what help you'll need."},{"to":"Patient (options)","s":"Living alone straight away may be hard because your wife did the meals, money and tablets. Options include a flat with carers visiting, housing with staff on site, or a care home with your own room. What sounds most like you?"},{"to":"Ward social worker","s":"He can't return home due to a court order. He needs a Care Act assessment, a housing referral under the duty to refer, and a plan with the alcohol service. Police and his wife's IDVA need his discharge address and date."}],"write":"14:40 Discharge planning. 74M, depression in remission, alcohol use disorder.\nInformed of court order preventing return home. Shocked, tearful, denied past violence, then accepted drinking ~50 units/week. Believes wife has been unfaithful for years; no plan to approach her.\nADLs: washes and dresses independently; has never cooked, managed money or tablets. OT: safe with microwave only.\nWishes: own flat near son; reluctant about a care home; open to housing with staff on site.\nCapacity: understood and weighed options; capacity for accommodation decision presumed.\nPlan: Care Act assessment (SW), housing referral (duty to refer) with consent, extra care housing preferred, interim residential if not ready. Alcohol liaison referral. Repeat ACE-III. Discharge details shared with police and IDVA. LPA discussed.\nDr A. Ade CT1 bleep 4412","make":["Confirm the order's terms and any bail conditions with police or the trust legal team before the conversation.","Ward social worker: Care Act needs assessment and housing referral; consider interim placement while housing is found.","Occupational therapy: kitchen, money and medication assessments; dosette or blister pack if needed.","Alcohol: AUDIT score, alcohol liaison or community alcohol service referral before discharge; relapse prevention plan.","Cognition: repeat ACE-III or MoCA after abstinence; refer to memory service if impaired.","Safeguarding: DASH risk assessment and MARAC referral if high risk; tell the police and his wife's IDVA the discharge date and address.","Discharge meeting with him, son (with consent), social worker, care coordinator and community team."],"next":"He chooses an extra care housing flat near his son. While it is arranged he goes to an interim residential home. The community older adult team follow him up, and the alcohol service sees him in his first week.","marks":["Checked the legal facts before speaking.","Gave the news clearly and allowed his reaction.","Explored daily living, money, medication and alcohol.","Offered realistic options with pros and cons and sought his preferences.","Linked discharge planning to safeguarding his wife.","Named who does what in the plan."],"pitfalls":["Avoiding the news or letting someone else break it.","Arguing about whether he was violent.","Assuming a care home without asking his wishes.","Forgetting alcohol relapse and his wife's safety in the plan."],"refs":["1. Care Act 2014 and Care and Support Statutory Guidance (DHSC; checked Nov 2026)","2. Homelessness Reduction Act 2017, duty to refer (checked Nov 2026)","3. Domestic Abuse Act 2021 and statutory guidance (Home Office; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG27 Transition between inpatient hospital settings and community or care home settings for adults with social care needs (2015; checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE CG115 Alcohol-use disorders guideline (2011; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, social care (carers, care homes, housing support) is arranged by the local authority, not the NHS, and is means-tested. The ward social worker is your key partner; involve them early, not on discharge day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026","obj":["Told him clearly about the court order and allowed time for his reaction","Recorded his abilities with cooking, money, medicines and self-care, and his drinking","Offered at least three realistic accommodation options and recorded his preference","Agreed referrals for a Care Act assessment, housing, alcohol support and repeat cognitive testing","Shared discharge details with police and his wife's IDVA as part of the safeguarding plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She wouldn't do that. That's my house. I paid for it.\"","r":"\"I can see this is a huge shock, and I'm sorry to be the one telling you. The court has said you can't return for now. Let's think about where you can be settled and safe.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"She's been carrying on behind my back. This is her and her fancy man.\"","r":"\"You sound very hurt and angry. I'd like to understand how long you've had those worries about her. Whatever has happened, the order stands, and going near her would get you into serious trouble.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've never cooked a meal in my life. I'll starve.\"","r":"\"That's an honest worry, and an important one. That's exactly why we'd arrange support, whether meals delivered, carers, or a place with staff. You won't be left to manage alone.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going in a home. That's where you go to die.\"","r":"\"I hear that. A care home isn't the only option. There are flats with staff on site, where you have your own front door. What would matter most in a new place?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I stop drinking, can I go home?\"","r":"\"Stopping drinking would be good for you, and we'll help. But the court order isn't up to us, so it won't change by itself. Let's plan for now, and you can get legal advice about the order.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I didn't mean to hurt her. I don't even remember.\"","r":"\"It sounds like that weighs on you. Not remembering is something we should look into, along with the drinking. Taking that seriously is part of making sure it doesn't happen again.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue mirtazapine 30 mg at night; check sodium, weight and falls risk","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day while drinking risk remains; LFT and GGT before discharge","Relapse prevention: acamprosate or naltrexone discussed with the alcohol service, checking renal and liver function","Repeat ACE-III after six weeks' abstinence; memory service referral if impaired"],"psych":["Explore jealous beliefs over time; record them in the risk formulation","Alcohol liaison brief intervention now, then community alcohol service key worker"],"social":["Ward social worker: Care Act needs assessment and interim placement if needed","Housing referral to the local authority under the duty to refer, with consent","OT: kitchen, money and medication assessments; blister pack and meals service if needed","Discuss Lasting Power of Attorney for finance; Citizens Advice for benefits including Attendance Allowance"],"safety":["DASH risk assessment; MARAC referral if high risk; IDVA informed of discharge plan","Police informed of discharge date and address under bail or order conditions","Community older adult team follow-up within 7 days of discharge; review risk at the discharge meeting"]},"facts":[{"h":"Duty to refer","x":"Under the Homelessness Reduction Act 2017, hospitals must refer people who may be homeless to the local housing authority, with consent. Do it early, not on discharge day."},{"h":"Care Act assessment is a right","x":"Anyone who appears to need care and support must be assessed by the local authority. The ward social worker requests it."},{"h":"Presume capacity","x":"He is presumed to have capacity to decide where to live. Assess only if there is a reason to doubt it, and record the decision-specific assessment."},{"h":"Jealousy is a risk marker","x":"Persistent beliefs that a partner is unfaithful, with past violence, raise the risk of further harm. Record them and share within the safeguarding plan."},{"h":"Check memory after abstinence","x":"Retest cognition after several weeks without alcohol. Alcohol-related impairment can improve; persistent deficits change the level of support needed."}]}
);
